<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The binary-compatibility shim. AssemblyName is deliberately
    "Clearinet.CompatShim", NOT "Fiddler", see the ".NET Extension
    Compatibility Design" doc's "Don't get sued" decision (following the
    project plan's own tenet 5): shipping an assembly whose own identity
    presents itself, at the binary level, as the real Fiddler assembly was
    judged too much trademark exposure to ship, regardless of how the
    clean-room member surface itself was derived. This project's own honest
    name is used instead; real extensions compiled against the actual
    "Fiddler" assembly won't bind to this unmodified (see
    Clearinet.LegacyExtensionHost's own LegacyExtensionLoader and its
    assembly-mismatch diagnostic for what happens instead, and the design
    doc for the full reasoning and what a user can do about it).

    Renamed twice, not once: first from "Fiddler" to "Clearinet.Fiddler"
    (dropped the exact binary identity real extensions bind to), then from
    "Clearinet.Fiddler" to this project's current name (dropped "Fiddler"
    from the assembly's own identity entirely, leaving only nominative
    references: prose describing compatibility, diagnostic messages that
    name "Fiddler" to explain a mismatch, the same kind of descriptive
    use a compatible third-party accessory makes when it says what it's
    compatible with). See the design doc for the full reasoning.

    Deliberately NOT strong-named (no SignAssembly/key file): unrelated to
    the identity question above, an unsigned assembly is still required
    for anyone who does recompile or re-target a real extension against
    this shim, since all five real compiled extensions this project
    inspected reference an unsigned "Fiddler" assembly, and a
    strong-named replacement wouldn't satisfy that shape either.

    net48, not net10.0: this assembly exists specifically because
    System.Windows.Forms.MenuItem/MainMenu/ContextMenu/StatusBarPanel
    were removed outright from WinForms on .NET 5+ (confirmed via
    Microsoft's own WinForms breaking-change docs and a real
    TypeLoadException report; see the design doc). Only actual .NET
    Framework still has them, which is the entire reason this lives in
    a separate legacy host process rather than CLeARINET's main
    Avalonia/.NET 10 app.

    Every member below is reproduced from this project's own read-only
    ECMA-335 metadata inspection of five real compiled extension .dll's
    (AustralianImages, ContentBlock, JSFormat, SAZClipboard, Differ):
    method/field names, parameter types, and return types only. No IL
    method body, decompiled logic, or .cs source file was used to write
    any of these members; see the design doc's clean-room section and
    each file's own remarks for exactly which sample(s) each member
    traces back to.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net48</TargetFramework>
    <OutputType>Library</OutputType>
    <AssemblyName>Clearinet.CompatShim</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Clearinet.CompatShim</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyVersion>2.4.2.5</AssemblyVersion>
    <!-- SDK-style csproj defaults LangVersion low for net48 targets unless told otherwise. -->
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>disable</Nullable>
    <GenerateAssemblyInfo>true</GenerateAssemblyInfo>
    <!-- Never sign this: see the remarks above. -->
    <SignAssembly>false</SignAssembly>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Classic .NET Framework GAC references, not NuGet packages: the
         UseWindowsForms SDK property is for net5.0-windows+ TFMs and
         doesn't apply the same way under net48. -->
    <Reference Include="System.Windows.Forms" />
    <Reference Include="System.Drawing" />
    <!-- For SazArchive.cs's real .saz read/write: on classic .NET
         Framework the static System.IO.Compression.ZipFile convenience
         class (OpenRead/Open/etc.) lives in a separate assembly, apart from
         ZipArchive itself; both are needed explicitly, since this SDK-style
         net48 project's implicit default references aren't guaranteed to
         include either. -->
    <Reference Include="System.IO.Compression" />
    <Reference Include="System.IO.Compression.FileSystem" />
    <!-- For parsing/writing _m.xml session metadata via XDocument/XElement. -->
    <Reference Include="System.Xml.Linq" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
